#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/chapter.rst:2
msgid "WebGIS related standards and specifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/chapter.rst:4
msgid "WebGIS service standards are the basis of modern WebGIS technology."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/chapter.rst:6
msgid ""
"In order to realize the interoperability of spatial data and promote the "
"sharing of spatial data among heterogeneous platforms, OGC (Open "
"Geospatial Consortium, Open Geospatial Consortium) has been committed to "
"providing standardization of software, data and services in the "
"geographic information industry, and has formulated a series of spatial "
"The service specification of the data, such as WMS, WMTS, WFS, etc. Based"
" on the OGC standard specification, the global sharing and "
"interoperability of geographic information has become an inevitable trend"
" in the development of GIS technology. Therefore, at present, most of the"
" open map service resources are released based on the OGC service "
"specification, providing standard OGC map services to the public."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:4
msgid "WebGIS related standards of OGC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:7
msgid ""
"OGC stands for Open Geospatial Consortium-OGC, which Committed to the "
"standardization of software, data and services in the geographic "
"information industry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:11
msgid ""
"OGC was named Open GIS Consortium from 1994 to 2004, and later changed "
"its name due to business needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:14
msgid ""
"OGC is a non-profit voluntary international standardization organization "
"that leads the development of spatial geographic information standards "
"and basic positioning services. In the field of spatial data "
"interoperability, the interoperability method based on the public "
"interface access mode is a basic operation method. Through the "
"International Organization for Standardization (ISO/TC211) or technical "
"consortium (such as OGC) to develop interface specifications for spatial "
"data interoperability, GIS software vendors develop spatial data read and"
" write functions that follow this interface specification, which can "
"realize the interoperability of heterogeneous spatial databases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:23
msgid ""
"The standards formulated and promoted by OGC include WMS, WMTS, WFS, WCS,"
" CSW, WPS and other Web service specifications, as well as related GML, "
"KML and GeoRSS specifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:28
msgid "Introduction of relevant standardization organizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:31
msgid "**OGC (Open Geospatial Consortium):**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:33
msgid ""
"It is an international organization specializing in the standardization "
"of international standards for geospatial information technology. It was "
"established in 1994 and currently has more than 400 members, including "
"companies, research institutes, high schools and government agencies from"
" different countries and regions. One of the important work of OGC is the"
" OWS (OGC Web Services) research program, the purpose of which is to "
"establish a standard framework, enable geographic information to be "
"seamlessly used in a variety of Web, wireless, location-based services "
"and mainstream information technology applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:42
msgid ""
"**ISO/TC 211 (International Organization for Standardization Geographic "
"Information Technology Committee 211):**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:44
msgid ""
"ISO/TC 211 is a technical committee established by an international "
"organization in 1994 to develop standards for geospatial information. The"
" number of standards developed by TC 211 starts from 19101. In general, "
"ISO's standards are more abstract, while OGC's standards are relatively "
"more specific. In recent years, ISO/TC 211 has worked closely with the "
"OGC. For example, OGC's WMS standard is also an ISO standard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:50
msgid "**W3C (World Wide Web Consortium):**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:52
msgid ""
"W3C is an organization engaged in the standardization of information "
"technology for the World Wide Web. It was founded in 1994 at the MIT "
"Computer Science Laboratory by TimBemers-Lee, the father of the World "
"Wide Web. The World Wide Web Consortium has developed a series of "
"specifications, among which HTML, CSS, XML Schema, RDF, and SVG have been"
" published with a wide range of influences. The Basic GeoRSS "
"specification developed by the W3C is still in use, albeit slightly "
"outdated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:60
msgid "Use of Open Specifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:62
msgid "Here are two types of open specifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:65
msgid "(1) Open data format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:67
msgid ""
"If a data format has a complete description document, it can be read and "
"written by various GIS software, and the inventor of the format does not "
"claim any royalty rights, then the data format is open."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:70
msgid ""
"Open data formats in text format include KML (Keyhole Markup Language, "
"Keyhole Markup Language), GeoJSON and TopoJSON, etc. JPEG and PNG are "
"open data formats in raster."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:73
msgid ""
"ESRI Shapefile, referred to as Shapefile, is an open format for spatial "
"data developed by the Environmental Systems Research Institute (ESRI)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:76
msgid ""
"Currently, the file format has become an open standard in the geographic "
"information software community, demonstrating the importance of ESRI in "
"the global geographic information system market."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:79
msgid ""
"Shapefiles are also an important interchange format that enables data "
"interoperability between ESRI and other companies' products."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:82
msgid ""
"In contrast to this is ESRI's File Geodatabase, which is a closed format,"
" it cannot be opened and created with any software other than ESRI's "
"tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:86
msgid "(2)Open specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:88
msgid ""
"The most important open specifications in the GIS industry are a series "
"of specifications formulated by OGC. International Organization for "
"Standardization (ISO) Technical Committee 211 (TC211) is also one of the "
"most important spatial information standards organizations. At present, "
"ISO/TC 211 has completed or is developing more than 40 international "
"standards for geographic information, including \"Geographic Information "
"Reference Model\", \"Geographic Information Concept Model Language\" and "
"\"Geographic Information Terminology\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:95
msgid ""
"In addition, ESRI, developed in collaboration with several other "
"organizations, which is driving the use of the open GeoServices REST "
"specification. This specification provides a standard method for Web "
"clients to communicate with GIS servers using REST technology. Web "
"services published through ArcGIS for Server adhere to this "
"specification. This means that non-ESRI developers are free to create "
"applications to publish and access Web services that conform to the "
"standard. Although GeoServices REST was not adopted by the OGC, it is a "
"prime example of a voluntary disclosure specification for commercial "
"software."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:105
msgid "OGC map service standard introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:107
msgid ""
"OGC1999 began WMT1 (Web Map Tested) and WMT2 interoperability project. "
"Among them, the famous GML comes from the results of WMT1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:110
msgid ""
"In WMT2, OGC defines three geographic reference information models: Web "
"Map Server (WMS), Web Feature Server (WFS), Web Coverage Server (WCS)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:113
msgid "WMT1: http://www.opengeospatial.org/projects/initiatives/wmt1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:114
msgid "WMT2: http://www.opengeospatial.org/projects/initiatives/wmt2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:116
msgid ""
"Cyberspace data service is the most important functional service provided"
" by the data layer to the outside world. In order to realize spatial data"
" sharing and interoperability, the data layer will provide an "
"international standard access interface that conforms to the OGC "
"specification, adopt OWS service model to realize W*S service. Each "
"service type will conform to the latest protocols and specifications, "
"enabling visual access to map data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:122
msgid ""
"OGC map service protocols, including WMS, WFS, WCS, WMTS, WPS. One of the"
" more important standards that are now used more are GML, WMS and WFS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:126
msgid "Web Map Service (WMS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:128
msgid ""
"Web Map Service (WMS) can return corresponding maps (including raster "
"forms such as PNG, GIF, JPEG, or vector forms such as SVG and WEB CGM) "
"according to the user's request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:132
msgid ""
"WMS supports the network protocol HTTP, and the supported operations are "
"defined by URLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:138
msgid "WMS provides the following main operations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:140
#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:293
msgid ""
"``GetCapabitities`` : Returns service-level metadata, which is a "
"description of service information content and required parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:142
msgid ""
"``GetMap`` : Returns a map image whose geospatial reference and size "
"parameters are well-defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:143
msgid ""
"``GetFeatureInfo`` : Returns information about some special features "
"displayed on the map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:144
msgid "``GetLegendGraphic`` : Returns the legend information of the map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:147
msgid "Other operations like ``DescribeLayer`` , ``GetStyles`` , ``SetSytles`` ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:149
msgid ""
"In fact, to explain with the traditional point of view, what ``GetMap`` "
"obtains is the result drawn on the control in the desktop program, which "
"is the performance of the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:153
msgid ""
"``GetFeatureInfo`` is easier to understand. It has the same function as "
"the Info button used in almost all desktop programs, it is used to obtain"
" information about the coordinates of the screen somewhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:157
msgid ""
"The parameters in ``GetFeatureInfo`` are screen coordinates, current view"
" range, etc., which also facilitates the writing of the client to a "
"certain extent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:159
msgid ""
"``GetFeatureInfo`` can return feature information in multiple layers at "
"the same time, which is the same as ArcGIS Desktop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:165
msgid ""
"``GetFeatureInfo`` can return feature information in multiple layers at "
"the same time, which is the same as the operation in GIS desktop "
"applications. WMS also includes some ``GetLegend`` and other requests to "
"return legend information, which are also completely defined according to"
" the existing standards of the desktop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:170
msgid ""
"The full name of WMS is \"Web Map Service\". The standard mainly defines "
"three operations for creating and displaying map images: "
"``GetCapabilities`` (getting service capabilities), ``GetMap`` (getting "
"maps), and ``GetFeatureInfo`` (getting object information).  ``GetMap`` "
"is a common operation, which results in an image of a map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:176
msgid ""
"WFS is an online service standard for geographic elements based on Web "
"service technology. It has two functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:178
msgid ""
"One is to realize the Web service of geographic data. The data service "
"department has established a geospatial database system to provide online"
" services, and users can obtain the geospatial data they need through "
"this standard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:182
msgid ""
"The second is the specification for interoperability of heterogeneous "
"systems. Two different geographic information systems can realize the "
"interoperability of heterogeneous data, including data query, browsing, "
"extraction, modification, update and other operations. It can realize "
"remote interoperability based on Web technology."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:187
msgid ""
"Web Mapping Services (WMS) make maps from data with geospatial location "
"information. A map is defined as a visual representation of geographic "
"data. it can return the corresponding map (including PNG, GIF, JPEG and "
"other raster forms or SVG and WEB CGM and other vector forms) according "
"to the user's request. WMS supports the network protocol HTTP, and the "
"supported operations are defined by URLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:196
msgid "Web Feature Service (WFS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:198
msgid ""
"Web Feature Services (WFS) supports insert, update, delete, retrieve, and"
" discover services for geographic features. The service returns GML data "
"based on HTTP client requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:202
msgid ""
"Its basic connection is: GetCapabilities, DescribeFeatureType, GetFeature"
" GetCapabilities as above. DescribeFeatureType returns the feature "
"structure for client queries and other operations. GetFeature can return "
"a GML-compliant data document according to the query requirements. "
"GetFeature is the most important interface. Other interfaces such as "
"Transaction can not only provide element reading, but also support "
"element online editing and transaction processing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:210
msgid ""
"WFS corresponds to the conditional query function in common desktop "
"programs. WFS constructs query conditions through OGC Filter, supports "
"query based on spatial geometric relationship, query based on attribute "
"domain, and of course, includes common query based on spatial "
"relationship and attribute domain. On the Web, WFS requests are not "
"implemented in SQL, but through Filter XML, which is more scalable. What "
"WFS returns is the result set of the query, to a certain extent, it is "
"different from the \"data representation\" of WMS, The result set of WFS "
"is a result set defined and constrained by a complete Schema, with GML as"
" the carrier. This result set is similar to the data table of the query "
"results of the desktop program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:221
msgid "WFS provides the following operations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:223
msgid ""
"``GetCapabitities`` : Returns service-level metadata, which is a "
"description of service information content and required parameters  "
"(described in XML)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:225
msgid ""
"``DescribeFeatureType`` : Generate a Schema to describe the feature types"
" that the WFS implementation can provide. The Schema description defines "
"how the WFS implementation encodes a feature instance on input and "
"generates a feature instance on output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:228
msgid ""
"``GetFeature`` : A data document that conforms to the GML specification "
"can be returned according to the query requirements. GetFeature is the "
"most important interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:230
msgid ""
"``LockFeature`` : When the user requests through Transaction, in order to"
" ensure the consistency of the feature information. That is, when a "
"transaction accesses a data item, other transactions cannot modify the "
"data item and add element locks to the element data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:234
msgid ""
"``Transaction`` : Interaction with feature instances. This operation can "
"not only provide feature reading, but also support feature online editing"
" and transaction processing. The Transaction operation is optional, and "
"the server chooses whether to support this operation according to the "
"nature of the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:241
msgid ""
"The first three operations are required operations, which can obtain "
"geographic elements; the latter two are optional operations, which are "
"mainly used for adding, deleting, and modifying geographic elements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:245
msgid ""
"Web Feature Service (WFS) supports users to insert, update, delete, "
"retrieve and discover geographic features through HTTP in a distributed "
"environment. The service returns feature-level GML (Geography Markup "
"Language, Geographic Markup Language) data according to HTTP client "
"requests, and provides transaction operations such as adding, modifying, "
"and deleting features, which is a further development of Web map "
"services. WFS constructs query conditions through OGC Filter, supports "
"query based on spatial geometric relationship, query based on attribute "
"domain, and of course also includes common query based on spatial "
"relationship and attribute domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:257
msgid ""
"Other interfaces such as Transaction can not only provide element "
"reading, but also support element online editing and transaction "
"processing. WFS corresponds to the conditional query function in common "
"desktop programs. WFS constructs query conditions through OGC Filter, "
"supports query based on spatial geometric relationship, query based on "
"attribute domain, and of course, includes common query based on spatial "
"relationship and attribute domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:265
msgid ""
"On the Web, WFS requests are not implemented in SQL, but through Filter "
"XML, which is more scalable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:269
msgid ""
"What WFS returns is the result set of the query, to a certain extent, it "
"is different from the \"data representation\" of WMS, The result set of "
"WFS is a result set defined and constrained by a complete Schema, with "
"GML as the carrier. This result set is similar to the data table of the "
"query results of the desktop program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:277
msgid "Network Coverage Service (WCS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:279
msgid ""
"Web Geographic Coverage Service (WCS): Provides a spatial raster layer "
"containing geographic information or attributes, instead of static map "
"access. Send corresponding data according to HTTP client request, "
"including imagery, multispectral imagery and other scientific data. Two "
"important operations GetCapabilities, GetCoverage GetCapabilities returns"
" a description of the service and an XML document from which to obtain a "
"collection of covered data. GetCoverage is executed after GetCapabilities"
" determines the query scheme and the data to be acquired, and returns the"
" coverage data. There is also the optional operation "
"DescribeCoverageType."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:288
msgid ""
"WCS corresponds to the functions based on raster data, and corresponds to"
" the characteristics of WMS based on vector data. The network coverage "
"service is oriented to spatial image data. It exchanges geospatial data "
"containing geographic location as \"COverage\" on the Internet, such as "
"raster data such as satellite imagery and digital elevation data. WCS "
"provides the following operations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:295
msgid ""
"``DescribeCoverage`` : Allows users to obtain detailed description "
"documents of one or more coverages from a specific WCS server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:297
msgid ""
"``GetCoverage`` : Returns a response document containing or referencing "
"the requested coverage data according to the query request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:300
msgid ""
"Web Geographic Coverage Service (WCS): Provides access to spatial raster "
"layers containing geographic information or attributes rather than static"
" maps. Send corresponding data according to HTTP client request, "
"including imagery, multispectral imagery and other scientific data. Two "
"important operations are ``GetCapabilities``, ``GetCoverage``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:305
msgid ""
"``GetCapabilities`` returns a description of the service and an XML "
"document from which to get a collection of covered data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:307
msgid ""
"``GetCoverage`` is executed after GetCapabilities determines the query "
"scheme and the data to be acquired, and returns the coverage data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:310
msgid ""
"Also an optional action is ``DescribeCoverageType``. WCS corresponds to "
"the functions based on raster data, and corresponds to the "
"characteristics of WMS based on vector data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:314
msgid ""
"The Web Overlay Service (WCS) is oriented towards spatial imagery data, "
"which exchanges geospatial data containing geographic location values "
"with each other on the Internet as \"Coverage\". The network coverage "
"service consists of three operations: GetCapabilities, GetCoverage and "
"DescribeCoverageType. The GetCapabilities operation returns an XML "
"document describing the service and dataset. The GetCoverage operation in"
" the network coverage service is performed after GetCapabilities "
"determines what kind of query can be executed and what kind of data can "
"be obtained. It returns the value or attribute of the geographic location"
" using a common overlay format. The DescribeCoverageType operation allows"
" clients to request a full description of any coverage provided by a "
"specific WCS server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:324
msgid ""
"The above three specifications can not only be used as the spatial data "
"service specification of Web services, but also can be used as the "
"interoperability of spatial data to realize the Far East. As long as a "
"certain GIS software supports this interface and is deployed on the local"
" server, other GIS software can obtain the required data through this "
"interface. From the perspective of technical implementation, a Web "
"service can be understood as an application program that exposes an "
"interface that can be invoked through the Web to the outside world, "
"allowing it to be invoked by programs written in any platform, any "
"system, and in any language. This application can be implemented in a "
"variety of existing programming languages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:334
msgid ""
"The biggest feature of web services is that they can achieve cross-"
"platform, cross-language, and cross-hardware interoperability. It is "
"SOAP, WSDL and UDDI in web services that ensure the cross-platform "
"interoperability of web services. Therefore, how to use SOAP, WSDL "
"Deploying, describing, transporting and registering a Web service with "
"UDDI is the key to implementing Web services. Since SOAP, WSDL and UDDI "
"are a set of standards, different manufacturers can have different "
"products that implement these standards, such as the Web service toolkit "
"based on the JAVA platform launched by companies such as SUN, APACHE, "
"IBM, and Borland, and the .NET proposed by Microsoft. Platform, etc. "
"These tools provide convenient tools for the development, deployment and "
"description of Web services, which greatly reduces the complexity of "
"developing Web services."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:347
msgid "Tile Map Service (TMS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/cha2_ogc-opengis/sec01_ogc-standard/section.rst:349
msgid ""
"The Tile Map Service (TMS) defines operations that allow users to access "
"tile maps. WMTS may be OGC's first service standard to support RESTful "
"access."
